@@ -0,0 +1,116 @@
+# Dynamic columns (a runtime column set)
+
+> An entity can expose the **extra columns** of a result row as a dictionary whose keys are defined by the schema at run time rather than by CLR members. On read the store is filled with every column that is not mapped to a declared property; on write its keys are rendered as columns (always quoted).
+
+**Prerequisites:** [Entities and metadata](../getting-started/03-entities-and-metadata.md) · [Value converters and JSON columns](28-value-converters.md) · [Limitations](../advanced/limitations.md)
+
+A normal nextorm entity has a fixed set of mapped columns. A **dynamic-columns store** is the escape hatch for schemas that are not known at compile time (user-defined attributes, extension tables, EAV-style rows): the entity carries an `IDictionary<string, object?>` that receives the columns the result set has in addition to the declared ones, and whose entries are written back as columns.
+
+## Declaring the store
+
+Mark one writable property with [`DynamicColumnsAttribute`](xref:NextORM.Core.DynamicColumnsAttribute). The property must be assignable to `IDictionary<string, object?>` — typically `IDictionary<string, object?>` or `Dictionary<string, object?>`:
+
+```csharp
+[SqlTable("product")]
+public class Product
+{
+    public int Id { get; set; }
+    public string? Name { get; set; }
+
+    [DynamicColumns]
+    public Dictionary<string, object?> Attributes { get; set; } = new();
+}
+```
+
+The fluent equivalent is [`EntityPropertyBuilder<T>.DynamicColumns()`](xref:NextORM.Core.EntityPropertyBuilder`1.DynamicColumns):
+
+```csharp
+ctx.From<Product>(b => b
+    .Table("product")
+    .Property(p => p.Attributes).DynamicColumns());
+```
+
+A mapping may declare at most one store, and the store cannot be combined with a column name, a range pair or a value/JSON converter (it is not a physical column).
+
+## Reading
+
+When an entity declares a store, its `SELECT` projection selects the mapped columns **and a wildcard** (`select "Id", "Name", * from "product"`). The mapped columns keep fixed ordinals; the store is materialised from the wildcard columns whose names do not match a mapped column:
+
+```csharp
+var product = ctx.From<Product>().Where(p => p.Id == 1).Single();
+
+// The table also has the columns "color" and "weight":
+product.Attributes["color"];   // the extra columns are here
+product.Attributes["weight"];
+```
+
+The mapped columns are never copied into the store (`Id`/`Name` are absent from `Attributes`), and the comparison is case-insensitive.
+
+A raw command fills the same store from the columns it returns that bound no mapped property (see [Raw SQL](12-raw-sql.md)):
+
+```csharp
+using var result = ctx.ExecuteRaw("select id, name, color, weight from product");
+var products = result.Read<Product>();
+```
+
+The materialised entity needs a public parameterless constructor when it declares a store (the row is built by property assignment).
+
+## Writing
+
+The dynamic keys are written as columns by the entity-value forms:
+
+- `InsertInto<T>().Values(entity)` / `Values(entities)`,
+- `Update<T>().Set(entity)` and `Update<T>(entity)`,
+- the key-upsert `MergeInto<T>().Using(entity).OnKeys().WhenMatchedUpdate().WhenNotMatchedInsert()`.
+
+```csharp
+ctx.InsertInto<Product>()
+    .Values(new Product
+    {
+        Id = 1,
+        Name = "T-shirt",
+        Attributes = new Dictionary<string, object?>
+        {
+            ["color"] = "black",
+            ["weight"] = 0.35,
+        },
+    })
+    .Insert();
+// insert into product (Id, Name, "color", "weight") values ($p0, $p1, $p2, $p3)
+```
+
+For a multi-row insert/merge the column set is the **union** of the keys across the rows; a key missing from a particular row is written as `NULL`. A key that is present with a `null` value is also written as `NULL`.
+
+## Identifier safety
+
+A dynamic column name is runtime data, so it is **always quoted** using the active dialect's identifier quoter (`"..."` on PostgreSQL/SQLite, `[...]` on SQL Server, `` `...` `` on MySQL/MariaDB/ClickHouse) and the quote character in the name is escaped (`x"; drop table …` becomes `"x""; drop table …"`). This is independent of `DataContextBuilder.UseQuotedIdentifiers`. Names that are null/empty/whitespace, longer than 128 characters or contain a control character are rejected with a `BuildSqlCommandException`.
+
+## Plan key
+
+The dynamic store is part of the plan key. For a write the rendered statement includes the runtime column list, so two different key sets produce two different commands/plans. For a read the store's presence is part of the column's plan hash (a projection with the store never shares a plan with one without it), and a raw command's mapper is keyed by the ordered result-set column names, so a different extra-column set gets a different compiled mapper. Do not mutate a store while prepared commands over the entity are cached.
+
+## In-memory provider
+
+The in-memory provider returns the registered entity instance as is, so a store populated on the source entity is preserved on read; a key-upsert merge copies the source row's store entries into the matched instance.
+
+## Provider matrix
+
+| Provider | Read | Write |
+|---|---|---|
+| SQL Server | yes (`[...]` quoting) | yes |
+| PostgreSQL | yes (`"..."`) | yes |
+| MySQL / MariaDB | yes (`` `...` ``) | yes |
+| SQLite | yes (`"..."`) | yes |
+| ClickHouse | yes (`` `...` ``) | yes |
+| In-memory | the source entity's dictionary | key-upsert merge only |
+
+## Limitations
+
+The read wildcard expands every column of the `FROM` source, so a store-backed entity is meant to be queried as a plain single-source projection. Combining it with `DISTINCT`/`GROUP BY`/aggregates (where the wildcard becomes a grouping member) or with a join that exposes duplicate column names is not supported and may produce SQL the provider rejects. The full-`MERGE` **branch** form (`WhenMatched()/WhenNotMatched()`) writes only the declared columns of the branch; use the key-upsert form for dynamic columns. See [Limitations](../advanced/limitations.md#dynamic-columns).
+
+## See also
+
+- [Entities and metadata](../getting-started/03-entities-and-metadata.md)
+- [Value converters and JSON columns](28-value-converters.md)
+- [Raw SQL](12-raw-sql.md)
+- [Limitations](../advanced/limitations.md)
